## API Mocking with MSW

## Why Mock At The Network Level

```
Component → fetch('/api/questions') → [MSW intercepts] → mock response

Advantages:
  - No code changes in components (real fetch calls used)
  - Same mocks work in browser dev AND in tests (Node.js)
  - Mock realistic network delays, errors, loading states
  - Mocks serve as living documentation
```

## MSW Setup (Next.js)

```typescript
// mocks/handlers.ts
import { http, HttpResponse } from 'msw';

export const handlers = [
  http.get('/api/questions', ({ request }) => {
    const url = new URL(request.url);
    const topic = url.searchParams.get('topic');

    return HttpResponse.json({
      data: mockQuestions.filter(q => !topic || q.topic === topic),
      pagination: { total: 50, page: 1 },
    });
  }),

  http.post('/api/questions', async ({ request }) => {
    const body = await request.json();
    return HttpResponse.json({ id: 999, ...body }, { status: 201 });
  }),

  // Simulate error states
  http.get('/api/questions/999', () => {
    return HttpResponse.json({ error: 'Not found' }, { status: 404 });
  }),
];

// mocks/browser.ts (browser environment)
import { setupWorker } from 'msw/browser';
export const worker = setupWorker(...handlers);

// mocks/server.ts (test / Node environment)
import { setupServer } from 'msw/node';
export const server = setupServer(...handlers);
```

## Using In Tests

```typescript
