## Sync vs Async APIs

## Synchronous

```
Client → POST /reports/generate → Server (processes 30s) → 200 OK { data }
          ↑ blocked for 30 seconds

❌ HTTP timeout (default 30s Vercel, 60s other platforms)
❌ Client UI freezes
❌ Can't retry partial work on failure
❌ Server resources tied up for entire duration

Use for: queries < 1-2 seconds, CRUD, data retrieval
```

## Asynchronous — Polling

```
Client → POST /reports/generate → 202 Accepted { jobId: 'job_abc' }
Client → GET /reports/job_abc   → 200 { status: 'processing', progress: 45 }
Client → GET /reports/job_abc   → 200 { status: 'processing', progress: 82 }
Client → GET /reports/job_abc   → 200 { status: 'done', url: '/reports/result_abc.pdf' }

✅ No timeout risk
✅ Retry individual failed steps
✅ Server resources freed (job runs in worker)
✅ Progress reporting
```

## Asynchronous — Webhook

```
Client → POST /exports { callbackUrl: 'https://myapp.com/export-done' }
       → 202 Accepted { jobId }

[server processes in background]

Server → POST https://myapp.com/export-done { jobId, status: 'done', url: '...' }

✅ Client doesn't need to poll
❌ Client needs a public endpoint
```

## Implementation (Next.js + Background Worker)

```typescript
// Submit job
export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const body = await req.json();
  const jobId = crypto.randomUUID();

  await redis.set(`job:${jobId}`, JSON.stringify({ status: 'pending', progress: 0 }));
  await queue.add('generate-report', { jobId, ...body }); // BullMQ or similar

  return Response.json({ jobId }, { status: 202 });
}

// Poll status
export async function GET(req: Request, { params }: { params: { id: string } }) {
  const job = JSON.parse(await redis.get(`job:${params.id}`) ?? 'null');
  if (!job) return Response.json({ error: 'Job not found' }, { status: 404 });
  return Response.json(job);
}
```

