## Key Scenarios To Cover

```
✅ Happy path — valid input, expected output
✅ Missing auth → 401
✅ Insufficient permissions → 403
✅ Non-existent resource → 404
✅ Invalid body → 422 with field-level errors
✅ Duplicate resource → 409
✅ Server error → 500 (mock DB failure)
✅ Pagination — first page, last page, empty results
✅ Filter combinations
✅ Idempotency — duplicate POST with same idempotency key
```

## Gateway vs Application Rate Limiting

## Layer 1: Gateway (Edge)

```
Cloudflare WAF / AWS API Gateway / Nginx

✅ Applied before request hits your server
✅ Handles massive spike without consuming app resources
✅ Built-in DDoS mitigation
✅ No code required — configured in dashboard/IaC
❌ IP-based only (users behind NAT share a limit)
❌ Can't distinguish free vs paid users
❌ No business-context rate limiting

Typical config:
  100 req/min per IP to /api/*
  10 req/min per IP to /api/auth/login (brute force protection)
```

## Layer 2: Application (Redis)

```typescript
// Per-user rate limiting (not per-IP)
export async function rateLimit(userId: string, tier: 'free' | 'pro') {
  const limits = { free: 60, pro: 1000 }; // req/hour
  const key = `rl:user:${userId}:${new Date().getUTCHours()}`;

  const count = await redis.incr(key);
  if (count === 1) await redis.expire(key, 3600);

  return {
    limited: count > limits[tier],
    remaining: Math.max(0, limits[tier] - count),
    limit: limits[tier],
    reset: /* next hour */ new Date().setUTCMinutes(0, 0, 0) + 3600_000,
  };
}
```

## Business Quota (Monthly)

```typescript
// 100 AI-generated explanations per month for free tier
async function checkAiQuota(userId: string): Promise<boolean> {
  const key = `quota:ai:${userId}:${new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 7)}`; // YYYY-MM
  const count = await redis.incr(key);
  if (count === 1) await redis.expireat(key, endOfMonth());
  return count <= 100;
}
```

## Response Headers

```
X-RateLimit-Limit: 1000
X-RateLimit-Remaining: 847
X-RateLimit-Reset: 1700000000
X-RateLimit-Policy: 1000;w=3600  (RFC 6585 format)
```

## tRPC

## The Problem It Solves

```
Traditional REST:
  Server returns: { name: string, email: string }
  Client types:   { name: string, email: string }  ← manually duplicated

  When server changes 'email' to 'emailAddress':
  → TypeScript error on server ✅
  → Client still compiles fine (uses old type) ❌
  → Runtime error in production 💥

tRPC:
  Server defines return type once
  Client infers it automatically
  → Change on server = TypeScript error on client immediately ✅
```

## Setup (Next.js App Router)

```typescript
// server/routers/question.ts
import { z } from 'zod';
import { router, publicProcedure, protectedProcedure } from '../trpc';

export const questionRouter = router({
  getById: publicProcedure
    .input(z.object({ id: z.number() }))
    .query(async ({ input }) => {
      return db.question.findUniqueOrThrow({ where: { id: input.id } });
      // Return type is automatically inferred from Prisma
    }),

  create: protectedProcedure
    .input(CreateQuestionSchema)
    .mutation(async ({ input, ctx }) => {
      return db.question.create({
        data: { ...input, createdBy: ctx.session.userId },
      });
    }),
});

// Export type for client
export type AppRouter = typeof appRouter;
```

